> Background
> The United Kingdom is already feeling the strain of climate impacts. Today, the UK’s welfare is an estimated 2.4% lower, in GDP-equivalent terms, than it would have been without climate impacts (Rising et al. 2026). Widespread, strategic, and well-funded adaptation is needed to address the challenges of the coming decades and to achieve a Well-Adapted UK.
> The PROSPER project aims to provide actionable information to fiscal decision-makers on the physical risks of climate change and the potential for adaptation. The project’s work is built around four core practices:
> •	Synthesising the best available evidence: PROSPER engages with experts in climate, infrastructure, and the economy, and brings together multiple lines of evidence on risks and adaptation into a consistent framework.
> •	Policy-relevant scenarios and storylines: Workshops and an advisory group bring together multiple branches of government to construct common scenarios and specific storylines to address the questions decision-makers have.
> •	Fiscal, financial, and economic outcomes: Our outcomes quantify impacts on government balance sheets, monetary losses, and economically valued welfare losses, for direct comparison to the costs of adaptation investments.
> •	Interactive decision-support tools: PROSPER is building new tools and datasets designed for decision-makers to navigate the dimensions of risk and the many options available to proactively address them.
> This policy brief presents initial results for the UK, capturing economic risks and adaptation opportunities in agriculture, flood and coastal management, health, and labour productivity. We also approximate UK-wide risks from trade spillovers, catastrophic economic risks, and other missing channels. A second policy brief provides fiscal and monetary outcomes across domestic and international storylines.
> Reading this policy brief
> The next box describes the climate scenarios studied under PROSPER. It follows a template which is then repeated for each of the main risk channels, including a map of projected risks across the UK in 2050 and a summary table of damages. The damages are reported as the expected annual damages (EAD) for the two climate scenarios, Most Likely and High Risk. For the 2030s and 2050s, we also describe the damages associated with “peak events”—the 1-in-20 chance outcome for the worst year in each decade. The baseline risk estimates do not include adaptation beyond present day conditions, but we also show the benefits of adaptation investment at the level laid out in the National Adaptation Plan (NAP), when applied to the limited set of adaptation solutions studied here. The text descriptions for each risk generally present the current state of knowledge for the risk channel globally, followed by outcomes for the UK and the benefits of adaptation.
> Climate Scenarios
> 
> PROSPER explores risks across two climate scenarios: A Most Likely climate scenario and a High Risk climate scenario. Under the Most Likely climate scenario, greenhouse gases peak in the next 20 years, followed by a gradual decline. Temperatures continue to rise through the century, and with the added influence of global tipping points, global warming reaches 2.3 °C in 2050 and 2.7 °C in 2075. This scenario is not a high-mitigation scenario, but rather one where global cooperation continues to meet challenges, even as some countries achieve their climate goals.
> 
> The High Risk scenario represents a divided world where emissions continue to rise through 2100. Including the effects of tipping points, global warming reaches 2.5 °C in 2050 and 3.3 °C in 2075.
> 
> Temperatures in the UK increase more rapidly than the global average, reaching 2.5 °C in the 2050s under the Most Likely scenario and 2.9 °C under the High Risk scenario. Based on historical variability, individual years in the 2050s could reach 4.1 °C above pre-industrial averages. 	 
