> Agricultural Yields Losses
> 
> Agriculture in the UK faces the possibility of rapid reductions in productivity. Higher temperatures reduce yields for most crops, with total calorie production expected to decline 4.4% per °C of warming (Hultgren et al., 2025). If the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) weakens significantly, it could result in wide-spread drying, leaving much of the UK nonarable (Ritchie et al., 2020).
> 
> Under the High Risk scenario, AMOC weakening results in large losses across the East of England region, only partially offset by gains in some parts of Wales and the South West. By the 2050s, there is a 1-in-20 chance that decadal extremes (the “peak events”) exceed £10 billion pounds annually under the High Risk scenario.
> 
> Under PROSPER, we have studied the potential for soil management for climate adaptation for wheat, barley, and potatoes. The largest benefits are achieved through compacting soils, but cover crops, spring cultivation, and shallow ploughing also provided benefits. These actions provide the greatest benefits under extreme outcomes, reducing the losses by half (e.g., £5 billion protected compared to £10 billion) under the £110 million per year investment planned under the NAP.	 
