> All−source flooding
> 
> The largest future risks from climate change are associated with combined river, surface water, and coastal flooding, exacerbated by sea-level rise and storm surges (CCC, 2021). At current levels, one sixth of the housing stock is at risk of flood damages annually, with climate change expected to exacerbate these challenges (Environment Agency, 2009, Bates et al., 2023).
> 
> Our meta-analysis finds flooding risks widely distributed across the UK, with pockets of particularly high expected losses across England and Scotland. Expected annual damages increase from £3 billion per year today to £5 billion per year under the Most Likely scenario and £7 billion per year under the High Risk scenario. However, peak events are 10 times this level, already £60 billion for the 2030s.
> 
> Adaptation can reduce these losses significantly. The spending level of £1.5 to £2 billion per year described by the NAP is expected to reduce both annual and extreme risks by 40% [25 – 55%].
> 
> We also consider coastal inundation and storm surge risks associated with sea-level rise, a subset of all-source flooding. Sea-level rise is relatively unaffected by near-term climate policy, reaching around 0.4 m above a 1985-2005 baseline under either scenario. The resulting inundation results in population displacement, capital loss, wetland destruction, while the combination of storms and higher sea-levels results in large peak events. In the 2030s, the peak event is estimated at £37 billion, and it reaches £67 billion in the 2050s.	 
