> Heat-Related Mortality
> 
> As summer heat waves have grown more intense in recent years, heat-related mortality has climbed, particularly amongst older populations (Arbuthnott & Hajat, 2017). At the same time, Warmer temperatures across the UK are expected to have different effects, depending on the climate, with some cooler regions benefiting from warmer winters (Carleton et al., 2022). As a cooler country, the net impact of warming may be positive in the near-term.
> 
> We find a pattern consistent with this intuition. In the north of Scotland and parts of Northern Ireland, warmer winters are expected to save lives. Across England and Wales, however, warming during summers results in excess mortality, with the greatest losses in the southern England. Peak events may result in high mortality already, with excess mortality from an extreme year in the 2030s exceeding 10,000 people. By the 2050s, across the UK, summer excess deaths in the average year will exceed winter reductions in mortality.
> 
> Reducing this risk requires household investment on a large scale. We find that levels of adaptation investment from the NAP make little difference when applied to a combination of solar blinds and air conditioning.	 
