> Labour Productivity
> 
> The productive capacity of both indoor and outdoor workers can be impacted by rising temperatures (Rode et al., 2022). Outdoor occupations in construction, forestry, and agriculture are particularly exposed. As summer heatwaves become more prevalent and extreme, workers will need more frequent breaks and total productivity will decline.
> 
> We find that the largest losses in productivity occur in southern England, where temperatures are highest, but counties with high potential damages are found anywhere there are large cities. The scale of these damages is similar for both the Most Likely and High Risk scenarios, resulting in annual damages around £20 billion per year by the 2030s. Peak events can result in more than £60 billion in losses.
> 
> We also quantify these losses in terms of hours per person per year, relative to preindustrial conditions. These models suggest that the UK currently has slightly higher productivity than under preindustrial temperatures, due to more productive winters. These gains are offset by losses during summers, with the total benefit cut in half by 2075 (0.3 hours per person per year gained in 2030 to 0.15 hours per person per year gained in 2075).
> 
> We consider heat alerts, air conditioning, solar blinds, improved ventilation, and behavioural change as mechanisms for workers to adapt to higher temperatures. These rapidly pay for themselves, with benefit-cost ratios around 10x. The level of funding described under the NAP may be a significant underinvestment given the scale of potential benefits.
